borator with triage permission or higher, is not the App, and comes after the last triage `:done` label of the issue. An approval given earlier is left in place and ignored: the gate waits for a fresh one and does not escalate. An approval from anyone else adds `ard:needs-human` with a comment that does not name the actor. A human who adds `ard:worker:<role>` directly bypasses the gate and has approved by doing so. The gate does not enforce that `triage:engineer` ran: the lead routes and the gate applies its routing. In `--dry-run` the gate logs `triage.would_gate` and writes nothing; otherwise each action records a `gate` event.\n26\t+Before it asks for approval or starts a worker, the gate checks that `.ard/<n>/plan.md` exists on `main`. If it does not, the gate posts one comment, adds `ard:needs-human` and records a `gate` event with reason `no-plan`; a check that fails to run is retried on the next tick. Removing a role's request label by hand does not count as that role finishing. With `require_approval: true` (the Phase 1 default) the gate posts one \"ready for approval\" comment per lead job and waits. `ard:approved` is honoured only when its latest `labeled` event (reset by an `unlabeled`) is by a collaborator with triage permission or higher, is not the App, and comes after the last triage `:done` label of the issue. An approval given earlier is left in place and ignored: the gate waits for a fresh one and does not escalate. An approval from anyone else adds `ard:needs-human` with a comment that does not name the actor. A human who adds `ard:worker:<role>` directly bypasses the gate and has approved by doing so. The gate does not enforce that `triage:engineer` ran: the lead routes and the gate applies its routing. In `--dry-run` the gate logs `triage.would_gate` and writes nothing; otherwise each action records a `gate` event.\n27\t \n28\t **Order comes from config, not the lead.** A role can declare `after: [architect, product-manager]`; the controller holds its request until those roles, if requested, are done. A prerequisite is requested when its request label (`ard:pending` for the lead) or its `:running` label is present, and done when its `:done` label is present; one that was never requested does not hold. A held role is logged as `triage.held`, is not a candidate and does not count against the one-triage-job-per-issue rule. Config rejects a role that is after itself and any cycle. Engineer triage normally runs last so its steps reflect the design and requirements.\n29\t \n30\tdiff --git a/docs/runbooks/gate-2.md b/docs/runbooks/gate-2.md\n31\tindex e1ff918..975819b 100644\n32\t--- a/docs/runbooks/gate-2.md\n33\t+++ b/docs/runbooks/gate-2.md\n34\t@@ -87,6 +87,7 @@ eng-review, and a human merges it. About an hour and a few dollars.\n35\t    - a \"ready for approval\" comment. **Read `.ard/<n>/plan.md`**, then add\n36\t      `ard:approved` yourself (it counts only from a human with triage\n37\t      permission, added after triage finished)\n38\t+   - instead of that comment, a needs-a-human comment when `plan.md` is missing\n39\t    - `ard:worker:engineer:running`, a `worker/<n>-…` branch and a draft PR\n40\t    - `ard:review:eng-review` on the PR, then a PR review and\n41\t      `ard:review:eng-review:done` or `:changes`\n42\tdiff --git a/internal/servecmd/triage.go b/internal/servecmd/triage.go\n43\tindex 2dfb97c..de84552 100644\n44\t--- a/internal/servecmd/triage.go\n45\t+++ b/internal/servecmd/triage.go\n46\t@@ -1,9 +1,14 @@\n47\t package servecmd\n48\t \n49\t import (\n50\t+\t\"context\"\n51\t+\t\"fmt\"\n52\t+\n53\t \t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/agent\"\n54\t \t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/agent/claude\"\n55\t+\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/closeout\"\n56\t \t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/controller\"\n57\t+\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/gitx\"\n58\t \t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/triage\"\n59\t )\n60\t \n61\t@@ -32,5 +37,21 @@ func triageLoop(w *wi